*  GIT.FILEIDS(REPO, FILES, IDS) — the files this account carries, as itself.
*  Copyright (C) 2026 Gordon Heydon.  GPL-2.0-only (see LICENSE).
*
*  ONE PLACE THAT ASKS THE QUESTION, the same reason GIT.FURNIDS and
*  GIT.FILECLASS exist: `add` and `status` have to agree exactly, or the account
*  never reads clean.  When add stopped staging a file's own VOC pointer
*  (mv_git#131) and status did not know to, every one came back as
*  `?? VOC/PTRTEST` — untracked forever, because the thing reporting them was
*  the thing that would never stage them.  That is #51, #78 and #95 again.
*
*  AND IT HAS TO BE BASIC, not an engine op.  On UniVerse the engine is the
*  background process and has NO RECORD ACCESS AT ALL — mv_open, mv_read and
*  mv_filelist are fatal there (MVXGIT_NORECORDS: "an opcode that needs record
*  I/O must be served in BASIC instead").  So a rule the engine can answer from
*  its arguments belongs behind GIT.OBJ, as furniture does, and a rule that has
*  to READ THE ACCOUNT belongs here.  Both halves are one-place-only; only the
*  place differs (mv_git#133).
*
*  STATUS CANNOT DERIVE THIS FROM WHAT GIT TRACKS.  Its UFILES comes from the
*  index, so it holds the files already committed — which is a different set,
*  and the difference is exactly the interesting cases: a file created but not
*  yet committed is absent from it, and so is BP.O, whose records are never
*  staged at all.  Asked of the index, both looked like "not a file here" and
*  their pointers were reported.
*
*  FILES is `id<VM>type` for the caller that wants the type; IDS is the bare
*  ids, for a LOCATE inside a record loop; FURN is the furniture ids among the
*  candidates, which the caller needs anyway -- a master-file pointer to a file
*  the filter excluded points at nothing and must not be staged (mv_git#78);
*  and TYPEDROP is the ids a wholesale add drops BY TYPE.
*
*  ONE PASS, four answers.  Every one of them needs the same walk of the master
*  file, and each used to do its own -- or, worse, not have one and guess from
*  the index instead.  Collecting them together is what makes asking cheap
*  enough that both callers ask rather than deciding for themselves.
      SUBROUTINE GIT.MASTER(REPO, FILES, IDS, FURN, TYPEDROP)
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
      AM = CHAR(254) ; VM = CHAR(253)
      FILES = "" ; IDS = "" ; FURN = "" ; TYPEDROP = ""
*  NOT COMPILED ON MVX, and that is the same split the rest of this package
*  makes: on MVX `add` and `status` are the ENGINE (GITADDALL / the status walk
*  in mvxgit.c), which asks mv_filelist directly and needs nothing from here.
*  The BASIC walk exists for UniVerse and UniData, where the engine has no
*  record access at all.  A plain IFNDEF around the body, never an $ELSE arm:
*  a guard nested in an $ELSE is the case the preprocessor gets wrong.
$IFNDEF MVX
*     The master file, under the name this platform uses for it.  MVMASTER is
*     an EQU in the generated PLATFORM.H (mvx#142) -- VOC everywhere, MD on
*     jBASE -- so there is nothing to fall back to.  This used to try VOC and
*     then MD, which is a guess dressed as a fallback: it costs a failed OPEN
*     every call, and on a system where both names exist it silently takes
*     whichever opens first.
      OPEN MVMASTER TO FIMF ELSE RETURN
      FIALL = ""
      FIRAW = ""
      FITYPE = ""
*     A select-list VARIABLE and the LOOP/ELSE EXIT form — the spelling
*     GIT.STATUS and GITUDT.CHECKOUT settled on, which both platforms accept.
      FILIST = ""
      SELECT FIMF TO FILIST
      LOOP
         READNEXT FIVID FROM FILIST ELSE EXIT
         READ FIVREC FROM FIMF, FIVID THEN
*           Attribute 1 is the TYPE, but not always the type ALONE: UniVerse
*           appends the description a user may give at CREATE.FILE time, so a
*           described file reads "F Customer master" and an exact match against
*           "F" makes it invisible.  Take the first token, and trim.
            FIVT = TRIM(FIELD(FIVREC<1>, " ", 1))
*           EVERY record is a candidate for the TYPE question, not just the
*           file-type ones: it is what decides whether a verb, a keyword or a
*           remote pointer travels.  Attribute 1 goes over as-is -- the engine
*           takes the first token, because UniVerse appends the file description
*           a user may give at CREATE.FILE time ("F Customer master").
            FITYPE<-1> = FIVID : VM : FIVREC<1>
            IF FIVT = "F" OR FIVT = "DIR" THEN
               FIDP = FIVREC<2> ; FIDD = FIVREC<3> ; FILOC = 1
               IF INDEX(FIDP, "/", 1) OR FIDP[1,1] = "@" THEN FILOC = 0
               IF INDEX(FIDD, "/", 1) OR FIDD[1,1] = "@" THEN FILOC = 0
*              Every file-type id is a CANDIDATE for the furniture question,
*              local or not: the master file points at furniture that has no
*              file here (_ENCINFO_, _KEYSTORE_) and those pointers are
*              furniture too.  Only the local ones are files this account has.
               FIALL<-1> = FIVID : VM : FIVT
               IF FILOC THEN FIRAW<-1> = FIVID : VM : FIVT
            END
         END
      REPEAT
      CLEARSELECT
*     The furniture rules themselves are NOT here — they live in
*     mv_account_furniture() in the engine, one table per MV system, and
*     GIT.FURNIDS is the one place that asks for them (mv_git#81).
      FIFURN = ""
      CALL GIT.FURNIDS(REPO, FIALL, FIFURN)
      FURN = FIFURN
*     ...and the TYPE table, which is per MV SYSTEM and lived in the engine
*     while the BASIC carried a single hardcoded copy for all of them:
*     V/K/F/LF/DF/DIR/Q/X/R regardless of platform.  Right for the two that run
*     this walk today and wrong for the next one -- on jBASE mv_voc_class()
*     answers 0 for everything, so the engine would keep what the BASIC dropped,
*     on the same account (mv_git#114, #133).  The op reads no records, so
*     mvgitd can answer it on UniVerse just as CallC does on UniData.
      CALL GIT.OBJ("VOCDROP", 2, REPO, FITYPE, "", "", "", TYPEDROP)
*     ...and an OBJECT FILE, which UniVerse keeps beside the source: compiling
*     BP/GIT writes BP.O/GIT.  `BP.O` is an ordinary F record in the master file,
*     so this walk saw it as a file like any other and staged all 61 compiled
*     objects in it.  Nothing caught that except the NUL-content guess in `add`,
*     which is why removing that guess -- the correct thing to do, since it also
*     drops legitimate records -- broke UniVerse outright (mv_git#145).
*
*     The rule is the engine's: a file named <X>.O whose <X> is ALSO a file here
*     is an object file, and none of its records are content.  Named, not
*     guessed: an account may own a file called SOMETHING.O with no SOMETHING
*     beside it, and that one is theirs.
      FINR = DCOUNT(FIRAW, AM)
      FOR FIK = 1 TO FINR
         FIID = FIELD(FIRAW<FIK>, VM, 1)
         FIDROP = 0
         FIL = LEN(FIID)
         IF FIL > 2 AND FIID[FIL-1, 2] = ".O" THEN
            FIBASE = FIID[1, FIL-2]
            FOR FIJ = 1 TO FINR
               IF FIELD(FIRAW<FIJ>, VM, 1) = FIBASE THEN FIDROP = 1
            NEXT FIJ
         END
         IF FIFURN # "" THEN
            LOCATE(FIID, FIFURN; FIFP) THEN FIDROP = 1
         END
         IF NOT(FIDROP) THEN
            FILES<-1> = FIRAW<FIK>
            IDS<-1> = FIID
         END
      NEXT FIK
$ENDIF
      RETURN
      END
